      ],
      "cards": [
        {
          "title": "Period of a Pendulum",
          "formulas": [
            "T = 2π√(L/g)"
          ],
          "paragraphs": [
            "For small angles (< 15°), the period depends only on length and gravity — not on mass or amplitude. This is isochronism ."
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Energy Dynamics",
          "formulas": [
            "E _tot = K + P + E _thermal"
          ],
          "paragraphs": [
            "Energy transitions smoothly between Kinetic (K) and Potential (P). Air drag continuously dissipates mechanical energy into Thermal energy, so the Total line stays flat even as the pendulum slows."
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Large-Angle Correction",
          "formulas": [
            "T ≈ 2π√(L/g) · (1 + θ²/16)"
          ],
          "paragraphs": [
            "For angles above ~15°, the small-angle approximation breaks down. The true period increases slightly with amplitude."
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Explore",
          "formulas": [],
          "paragraphs": [
            "Try this: Set the drag coefficient to C _d = 0.00 for an ideal, non-damped pendulum and watch K and P swap forever with a flat Total line. Raise it to 0.47 (a real sphere in air) or 0.94 (twice normal air drag) to watch Thermal climb while Total stays flat — energy is conserved, just converted.",
            "Drag the bob to change the angle. Change gravity to see how a pendulum would swing on the Moon (1.6 m/s²) or Jupiter (24.8 m/s²)."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "graphs": [
        {
          "id": "energyGraphCanvas",
          "label": "Energy vs. Time",
          "width": 650,
          "height": 150,
          "legend": [
            {
              "label": "Potential",
              "color": "#4a6fa5"
            },
            {
              "label": "Kinetic",
              "color": "#e05252"
            },
            {
              "label": "Thermal",
              "color": "#d97706"
            },
            {
              "label": "Total",
              "color": "#22c55e"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "lessons": [
        {
          "id": "pendulum-period-vs-length",
          "sim": "pendulum",
          "title": "What controls a pendulum’s period?",
          "summary": "Predict, then measure how string length changes the time for one full swing.",
          "subject": "Physics",
          "level": "Intro",
          "minutes": 6,
          "tags": [
            "period",
            "isochronism",
            "experimental design"
          ],
          "setup": {
            "controls": {
              "drag": 0,
              "gravity": 9.8,
              "mass": 1,
              "angle": 10,
              "length": 1
            },
            "action": "pause"
          },
          "steps": [
            {
              "id": "intro",
              "type": "message",
              "title": "A question first",
              "body": "The **period** T is the time for one full back-and-forth swing.\n\nThe string is 1.00 m long and the pendulum is paused. Before we measure anything, make a prediction."
            },
            {
              "id": "predict",
              "type": "predict",
              "title": "Your prediction",
              "body": "If the string is **longer**, what happens to the period?",
              "choices": [
                {
                  "id": "longer",
                  "label": "A longer string gives a longer period",
                  "correct": true
                },
                {
                  "id": "same",
                  "label": "The period does not change"
                },
                {
                  "id": "shorter",
                  "label": "A longer string gives a shorter period"
                }
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Good — we will test that. The theory says T ≈ 2π√(L/g).",
                "any": "Keep your prediction in mind; you will measure it in a moment."
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "set-length",
              "type": "task",
              "title": "Set the length to 2.00 m",
              "body": "Use the **L** slider (or the arrow keys) until it reads 2.00 m.",
              "highlight": [
                "length"
              ],
              "allow": [
                "length"
              ],
              "freeze": true,
              "hint": "The slider is the row labelled L.",
              "when": {
                "control": "length",
                "near": 2,
                "tolerance": 0.02
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "release",
              "type": "task",
              "title": "Release the pendulum",
              "body": "Press **Resume** and let it swing. The simulation will time the period for you.",
              "highlight": [
                "release"
              ],
              "allow": [
                "release"
              ],
              "when": {
                "state": "paused",
                "equals": 0
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "observe",
              "type": "observe",
              "title": "Watch the period settle",
              "body": "Let it swing for a few cycles. The highlighted **Period** readout converges once a full swing has been timed.",
              "highlight": [
                "bob"
              ],
              "when": {
                "state": "period",
                "settles": true,
                "tolerance": 0.12,
                "minElapsed": 6
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "check-length",
              "type": "checkpoint",
              "title": "Check the length relationship",
              "question": "length"
            },
            {
              "id": "check-mass",
              "type": "checkpoint",
              "title": "What about mass?",
              "question": "mass"
            },
            {
              "id": "check-gravity",
              "type": "checkpoint",
              "title": "What about gravity?",
              "question": "gravity"
            },
            {
              "id": "conclude",
              "type": "conclusion",
              "title": "You tested it",
              "body": "With drag removed, L = 1.00 m gives a period near **2.0 s**, while L = 2.00 m gives near **2.8 s**.\n\nThe period grows with the **square root** of the length — and, as you can check by changing mass, it does not depend on the bob’s mass."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "id": "pendulum-period",
        "version": 1,
        "questions": [
          {
            "id": "length",
            "use": "both",
            "type": "single",
            "prompt": "For an ideal small-angle pendulum, increasing length while keeping gravity fixed makes the period…",
            "choices": [
              {
                "id": "increase",
                "label": "Increase"
              },
              {
                "id": "same",
                "label": "Stay the same"
              },
              {
                "id": "decrease",
                "label": "Decrease"
              }
            ],
            "correct": [
              "increase"
            ],
            "explanation": "T = 2π√(L/g). A longer pendulum takes longer to complete one full back-and-forth swing."
          },
          {
            "id": "mass",
            "use": "both",
            "type": "single",
            "prompt": "With no drag, at small angles, doubling the bob mass while keeping length and gravity fixed makes the period…",
            "choices": [
              {
                "id": "double",
                "label": "Double"
              },
              {
                "id": "same",
                "label": "Stay the same"
              },
              {
                "id": "half",
                "label": "Halve"
              }
            ],
            "correct": [
              "same"
            ],
            "explanation": "Mass cancels from the ideal pendulum equation. It is absent from T = 2π√(L/g); this claim assumes no drag."
          },
          {
            "id": "gravity",
            "use": "both",
            "type": "single",
            "prompt": "The same ideal small-angle pendulum is moved to a place with weaker gravity. Its period will…",
            "choices": [
              {
                "id": "shorter",
                "label": "Be shorter"
              },
              {
                "id": "longer",
                "label": "Be longer"
              },
              {
                "id": "same",
                "label": "Be unchanged"
              }
            ],
            "correct": [
              "longer"
            ],
            "explanation": "T is proportional to 1/√g. Weaker gravity gives a longer period, with length held fixed."
          },
          {
            "id": "scaling",
            "use": "assessment",
            "type": "single",
            "prompt": "If length is multiplied by four at fixed gravity, what happens to the ideal small-angle period?",
            "choices": [
              {
                "id": "four",
                "label": "It is multiplied by four"
              },
              {
                "id": "two",
                "label": "It is multiplied by two"
              },
              {
                "id": "half",
                "label": "It is divided by two"
              }
            ],
            "correct": [
              "two"
            ],
            "explanation": "The square root of four is two. Period scales with √L, not directly with L."
          },
          {
            "id": "dependencies",
            "use": "assessment",
            "type": "multi",
            "prompt": "Which changes increase the period of an ideal small-angle pendulum? Hold other variables fixed for each change.",
            "choices": [
              {
                "id": "length",
                "label": "Increase length"
              },
              {
                "id": "mass",
                "label": "Increase mass"
              },
              {
                "id": "gravity",
                "label": "Decrease gravity"
              },
              {
                "id": "angle",
                "label": "Increase amplitude from 5° to 10° (small-angle approximation)"
              }
            ],
            "correct": [
              "length",
              "gravity"
            ],
            "explanation": "In the small-angle approximation, only L and g set the period: increasing L or decreasing g increases T. Mass and small amplitude do not appear in this approximation."
          },
          {
            "id": "limits",
            "use": "lesson",
            "type": "multi",
            "prompt": "Which assumptions support using T = 2π√(L/g)?",
            "choices": [
              {
                "id": "small",
                "label": "Small swing angles"
              },
              {
                "id": "ideal",
                "label": "Negligible drag"
              },
              {
                "id": "heavy",
                "label": "A very heavy bob is required"
              }
            ],
            "correct": [
              "small",
              "ideal"
            ],
            "explanation": "The formula describes an ideal, small-angle pendulum. At large angles the period increases with amplitude; a heavy bob is not a requirement."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
