            {
              "id": "nRTproduct",
              "label": "nRT (L.atm)",
              "value": "3.69",
              "tone": "default"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "idealGasSub",
          "text": "PV = nRT",
          "label": "Live equation"
        }
      ],
      "cards": [
        {
          "title": "Ideal Gas Law",
          "formulas": [
            "PV = nRT"
          ],
          "paragraphs": [
            "Relates pressure (P), volume (V), number of moles (n), temperature (T), and the gas constant (R = 0.0821 L.atm.mol ^-1 .K ^-1 )."
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Boyle's Law",
          "formulas": [
            "P _1 V _1 = P _2 V _2"
          ],
          "paragraphs": [
            "At constant temperature and moles, pressure and volume are inversely proportional. Squeeze the gas (decrease V) and pressure goes up (increase P)."
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Charles's Law",
          "formulas": [
            "V _1 / T _1 = V _2 / T _2"
          ],
          "paragraphs": [
            "At constant pressure and moles, volume is proportional to temperature. Heat the gas (increase T) and it expands (increase V)."
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Gay-Lussac's Law",
          "formulas": [
            "P _1 / T _1 = P _2 / T _2"
          ],
          "paragraphs": [
            "At constant volume and moles, pressure is proportional to temperature. Heat the gas (increase T) and pressure rises (increase P)."
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Avogadro's Law",
          "formulas": [
            "V _1 / n _1 = V _2 / n _2"
          ],
          "paragraphs": [
            "At constant pressure and temperature, volume is proportional to the number of moles. More particles (increase n) take up more space (increase V)."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "graphs": [],
      "lessons": [],
      "info": {
        "boyle": {
          "title": "Boyle's Law",
          "formula": "P₁V₁ = P₂V₂",
          "explanation": "At constant temperature and moles, the pressure and volume of a gas are inversely proportional. When you squeeze a gas into a smaller space, its pressure increases proportionally.",
          "detail": "Boyle's law describes how pressure and volume relate at constant n and T: P ∝ 1/V. If you double the volume, pressure halves; if you halve the volume, pressure doubles. This assumes the gas behaves ideally (no intermolecular forces and point-mass particles)."
        },
        "charles": {
          "title": "Charles's Law",
          "formula": "V₁ / T₁ = V₂ / T₂",
          "explanation": "At constant pressure and moles, the volume of a gas is proportional to its absolute temperature. Heating a gas causes it to expand; cooling causes it to contract.",
          "detail": "Charles's law states that volume is directly proportional to absolute temperature at constant n and P: V ∝ T. At absolute zero (0 K), the volume of an ideal gas would theoretically reach zero. This is why temperature must be measured in Kelvin."
        },
        "gaylussac": {
          "title": "Gay-Lussac's Law",
          "formula": "P₁ / T₁ = P₂ / T₂",
          "explanation": "At constant volume and moles, the pressure of a gas is proportional to its absolute temperature. Heating a gas in a rigid container increases the pressure.",
          "detail": "Gay-Lussac's law describes the direct proportionality between pressure and absolute temperature at constant n and V: P ∝ T. This explains why aerosol cans warn against heating - the pressure inside can rise until the container fails."
        },
        "avogadro": {
          "title": "Avogadro's Law",
          "formula": "V₁ / n₁ = V₂ / n₂",
          "explanation": "At constant pressure and temperature, the volume of a gas is proportional to the number of moles. More gas particles require more space at the same pressure.",
          "detail": "Avogadro's law states that equal volumes of gases at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of molecules: V ∝ n. At STP (0 °C, 1 atm), one mole of any ideal gas occupies 22.4 L."
        }
      }
    },
